WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.961
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.961 --> 00:00:10.249
Thank you, so about two years ago today,
after I'd first moved out to the US,

00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:14.260
I went up to Tahoe with
a group of GSB classmates.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.639
And the GSB really started our
immense carry-on with the holiday.

00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:23.825
But when we were up there, I remember
walking through the beautiful mountain

00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:28.850
air overlooking the lake, when suddenly
the silence and the bird song were

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:34.300
shattered by someone shouting, you are
such an asshole, you can say that here!

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.985
I was a little bit shocked.

00:00:35.985 --> 00:00:39.264
Someone was shouting at me, why?

00:00:39.264 --> 00:00:41.884
Because I had just said that I voted for
Brexit.

00:00:43.613 --> 00:00:48.376
Now I have to say I felt pretty taken
aback by someone that I'd really

00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:53.392
only just met, who was from Boston and
had really zero affiliation with

00:00:53.392 --> 00:00:58.510
the UK at all getting angry at me for
how I voted in the Brexit referendum.

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:03.963
That it made me realize that Brexit
is a controversial topic and

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:08.408
engenders a lot of negative
stereotypes and negative emotions.

00:01:08.408 --> 00:01:14.151
And this guy had made an assumption about
me that really was totally incorrect and

00:01:14.151 --> 00:01:16.865
misconstrued why I voted for Brexit.

00:01:16.865 --> 00:01:20.429
And so I want to use this opportunity
today to tell you about why I did vote

00:01:20.429 --> 00:01:21.100
for Brexit.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:27.630
I voted for Brexit because I want
Britain to be more open, more global.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.060
I've not voted for Brexit because I
want Britain to close in on itself.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.600
And I'm worried that all the bright,
intelligent people here at Stanford and

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.370
across the rest of the world will be
scared about going, and living, and

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:41.340
working, and

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.120
investing in the UK because of those
negative stereotypes about Brexit.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.820
I look forward to a post-Brexit
Britain that's more open, more global,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.020
and more inclusive, than it was before.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.280
And I want you guys all to be
excited about that, as well.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.900
So let me start off by putting
the EU in an American context.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:09.420
In my mind, the European Union
can be split into two parts,

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.570
a political union and an economic union.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.480
So if we apply this to the Americas,

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.080
if we apply an economic union, imagine
a federation of all 23 countries in

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.500
the whole of the Americas from Canada
all the way through to Argentina.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.700
Trade would be free and tariff-free.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.807
Argentinian steaks could be enjoyed
in Anchorage in the same way

00:02:30.807 --> 00:02:34.710
that the Alaskan oil and
gas could be traded in Buenos Aires.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:39.340
People would also be able to move freely,
traveling wherever they liked to live,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.650
to work, to study without discrimination.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.238
How many of you in this room,
and I want to see some hands,

00:02:46.238 --> 00:02:49.118
how many of you in this room would vote
for such a system in the Americas?

00:02:51.268 --> 00:02:55.783
So a few of you,
now think about the political union.

00:02:55.783 --> 00:03:02.910
Under the political union, we take a new
parliament and paste it, say, in Peru.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.810
And this would supersede the US House and

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.630
Senate and would start to make the
majority of the rules that govern the US.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.905
Now for the Americans of you in the room,
I'm sure you all love and

00:03:13.905 --> 00:03:17.701
you all respect the Supreme Court, but
we're going to replace that, as well.

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:20.883
We're going to put a new court,
say, in Belize.

00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:24.415
And this court is going to be staffed with
judges from across the Americas and will

00:03:24.415 --> 00:03:28.660
make the majority of the legal decisions
that mattered to the United States.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.655
Now how many of you would vote for
that system?

00:03:32.907 --> 00:03:35.710
So I don't see any hands.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:39.546
And that, for me,
is what the European Union is about.

00:03:41.935 --> 00:03:46.491
Now people in this room, I'm sure you
like Mexicans, you like the Canadians,

00:03:46.491 --> 00:03:50.175
especially if they look like
Justin Trudeau or Rachel McAdams.

00:03:50.175 --> 00:03:52.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:57.301
you do not want the good people of
Ottawa to be telling you what to do.

00:03:57.301 --> 00:04:01.190
You don't want the good people of Ottawa
to be telling you how many hours you

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.287
should be allowed to work in a given day,

00:04:03.287 --> 00:04:06.557
whether your prisoners should
be allowed to vote or not, and,

00:04:06.557 --> 00:04:10.290
most bizarrely of all, the size and
shape that your bananas should be.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.340
Now those are all things that the EU does.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:15.550
And so for me,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:20.120
Britain leaving the European union is
about leaving that political union.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.100
Taking back those decisions to the UK is
not about leaving the economic union.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:30.870
I think leaving the economic union of
the European Union would be bad for

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.070
both Britain and for
the rest of the world.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.290
It's going to make people poorer if
Germans can't sell their cars into

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:41.760
the UK and we can't sell our scotch
whiskey into the rest of Europe.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.903
But by leaving the political union,
I hope that we can expand our trade

00:04:47.903 --> 00:04:52.664
beyond just the European boundaries and
embrace more global trade.

00:04:52.664 --> 00:04:57.560
At the moment, global trade deals have
to be negotiated at a European level.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.390
Britain is not free to go
negotiate trade deals on its own.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.740
And that results in complications.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.290
Within Europe, there's lots of different
countries with lots of different economies

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.020
and different motives and interests.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.430
And those trade deals, as a consequence,
take a long time to negotiate.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.730
The most recent one was with Canada.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:18.420
And that took seven years for
the European Union to negotiate that deal.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:25.580
My hope is that Britain can embrace the
trading networks that we historically had.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:31.740
Britain has a great commonwealth and, for
a long time, traded around the world.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:32.941
In the 1960s and

00:05:32.941 --> 00:05:37.600
70s, when Britain first applied to
join the European Economic Community,

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.071
which is the precursor of the EU,
it was actually vetoed.

00:05:41.071 --> 00:05:43.637
Us joining was vetoed by the French.

00:05:43.637 --> 00:05:48.076
And the French reason was precisely
because they believed that Britain's

00:05:48.076 --> 00:05:52.450
trading networks were to too far-flung,
distant, and diverse lands.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:58.485
So I believe that by leaving the European
Union, Britain can re-embrace and

00:05:58.485 --> 00:06:02.391
reestablish those historical trading
networks, embracing the world,

00:06:02.391 --> 00:06:03.779
not closing in on itself.

00:06:05.911 --> 00:06:09.270
Now embracing the world does
not just mean economically.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.190
It also means embracing
the world's people.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.100
When I moved to the US two years ago,

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:21.450
it was the first experience I'd ever had
of being an immigrant to another country.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:25.580
And I realized quickly how difficult and
how many difficult decisions you end up

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.362
being forced to make when
you move to somewhere else.

00:06:28.362 --> 00:06:35.700
The hardest one for me involved my
girlfriend at the time, called Sophie.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.510
Now Sophie and I had gone out for
two and a half years.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.544
And I loved her very much.

00:06:41.544 --> 00:06:45.646
But when I got the phone call from
Derrick saying that I got into Stanford,

00:06:45.646 --> 00:06:50.408
the six months following that, the elation
of getting into Stanford, and coming and

00:06:50.408 --> 00:06:55.302
living in California, and meeting all you
amazing people, that elation slowly turned

00:06:55.302 --> 00:06:59.933
to despair as we realized that the US visa
rules meant it'd be almost impossible for

00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:01.490
Sophie to move out with me.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:06.120
Now I ended up, obviously,
coming, and Sophie stayed behind.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.098
And unfortunately, the 12-hour flight and

00:07:09.098 --> 00:07:13.648
the 8-hour time difference was too
much for our relationship to bear.

00:07:13.648 --> 00:07:18.492
That struck me as being incredibly
unfair that bright, young,

00:07:18.492 --> 00:07:22.439
ambitious people could not come and
work in the US and

00:07:22.439 --> 00:07:26.313
could not come and
contribute to the economy here.

00:07:26.313 --> 00:07:29.819
But unfortunately,
the same is true of the UK and for

00:07:29.819 --> 00:07:33.257
Americans wanting to go and
work and live in the UK.

00:07:33.257 --> 00:07:38.102
You would find it extremely difficult
when Britain is part of your,

00:07:38.102 --> 00:07:40.232
to go and move and work there.

00:07:40.232 --> 00:07:43.783
And the reason is is that you would be
behind the queue of people from Poland,

00:07:43.783 --> 00:07:47.057
from France, from Italy that are all
entitled to come, and work, and

00:07:47.057 --> 00:07:47.800
live in the UK.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.550
By having open borders to the whole
of Europe, you build walls for

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.010
the rest of the world.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:56.480
And I want those walls to come down.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:03.200
Britain should be open to accepting
the Ben's and Sophie's of China, of USA,

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.620
of India, of Africa and accepting as many
bright and brilliant people as we can.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.491
We should not just be restricted
to the Benjamin's, Bonjaman's,

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:13.036
and Benzino's of Europe.

00:08:18.651 --> 00:08:24.315
So for me, opening up to the world
is really what Brexit is about.

00:08:24.315 --> 00:08:25.780
It's not about closing in.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:32.879
And if I go back to that time in Tahoe,
I wish I hadn't been so taken aback.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.070
There are fundamental misconceptions and
stereotypes

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.410
that are applied to people that voted for
Brexit, but Brexit is going to happen.

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:46.120
And it's time that people that voted for
Brexit to stop feeling demonized for

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.000
the way they voted and start coming out
and presenting a positive image for

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.117
Brexit and for post-Brexit Britain.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.730
For my part, Brexit for

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.710
me was about opening Britain up
to the world, not closing us in.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:05.760
I'm excited about the future
of post-Brexit Britain

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.010
as a hub of globalization, accepting
brilliant people from around the world.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.865
And I hope some of you will come join us,
thank you.

00:09:14.865 --> 00:09:17.069
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:17.069 --> 00:09:21.852
[MUSIC]

